{{also|Devise|devisé}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|devisen}}, {{m|enm|devysen}}, from {{der|en|fro|deviser}}, from {{der|en|VL.|devisō}}, from {{der|en|la|dīvisō}}, frequentative of {{m|la|dīvidō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-devise.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪz|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|devis}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To use one's [[intellect]] to [[plan]] or [[design]] (something).
#: ''to '''devise''' an argument; to '''devise''' a machine, or a new system of writing''
#* Bancroft
#*: '''devising''' schemes to realize his ambitious views
#* {{quote-book|year=1988|author=Andrew Radford|title=Transformational Grammar|location=Cambridge|publisher=University Press|page=23|isbn=0-521-34750-5}}
#*: Thus, the task of the linguist '''devising''' a grammar which models the linguistic competence of the fluent native speaker is to '''devise''' a ''finite'' set of rules which are capable of specifying how to form, interpret, and pronounce an ''infinite'' set of well-formed sentences.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[leave]] ([[property]]) in a [[will]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To form a scheme; to lay a plan; to contrive; to consider.
#* Alexander Pope
#*: I thought, '''devised''', and Pallas heard my prayer.
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To plan or scheme for; to plot to obtain.
#* Spenser
#*: For wisdom is most riches; fools therefore / They are which fortunes do by vows '''devise'''.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[imagine]]; to [[guess]].
#: {{rfquotek|Spenser}}

